# Rank 1372 / CVE-2026-46519 (GHSA-cr22-wjx7-2w6m, CWE-863):
# Flux159/mcp-server-kubernetes exposes kubectl and helm operations to an LLM over
# MCP, and documents three server-side restrictions on which of those operations a
# client may reach: ALLOWED_TOOLS (a comma-separated allowlist),
# ALLOW_ONLY_READONLY_TOOLS, and ALLOW_ONLY_NON_DESTRUCTIVE_TOOLS. All three were
# applied in exactly one place -- the `ListToolsRequestSchema` handler, which
# computes the catalogue the server *advertises*:
#
#   server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => {
#     let tools;
#     if (allowedToolsEnv) { ... tools = allTools.filter(...) }
#     else if (allowOnlyReadonlyTools) { tools = readonlyTools; }
#     else if (nonDestructiveTools) { tools = allTools.filter(...) }
#     else { tools = allTools; }
#     return { tools };
#   });
#
# `tools/list` is advertisement, not authorization: MCP places no constraint on
# what tool name a `tools/call` request may carry, and the client is the untrusted
# side. The `CallToolRequestSchema` handler destructured `request.params` and
# dispatched straight into the Kubernetes operation --
#
#   const { name, arguments: input = {} } = request.params;
#   if (name === "kubectl_context") { return await kubectlContext(k8sManager, input); }
#   ...
#   if (name === "kubectl_delete") { return await kubectlDelete(k8sManager, input); }
#
# -- with no reference to any of the three restrictions. A client that knew or
# guessed a withheld tool's name invoked it, so a server started with
# ALLOW_ONLY_READONLY_TOOLS=true still served kubectl_delete, kubectl_generic,
# helm uninstall, cleanup and node drain. The weakness is not a missing check: the
# check exists, and is enforced at the wrong layer -- catalogue enumeration
# instead of dispatch.
#
# 461b50c5beff2fbcba79e07f3ecb95746b82a093 ("Enforce tool restrictions at
# tools/call layer") centralizes all three into one predicate and gates the call
# handler with it:
#
#   const isToolAllowed = (name: string): boolean => {
#     if (explicitlyAllowedToolNames) { return explicitlyAllowedToolNames.has(name); }
#     if (allowOnlyReadonlyTools) { return readonlyTools.some((t) => t.name === name); }
#     if (nonDestructiveTools) { return !destructiveTools.some((dt) => dt.name === name); }
#     return true;
#   };
#   ...
#   if (!isToolAllowed(name)) {
#     throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InvalidRequest, `Tool '${name}' is not allowed ...`);
#   }
#
# The pool row's metadata flag is `COMMIT_NONE` -- the advisory names no commit --
# but the row's commit is the real fix (41 changed lines in src/index.ts plus a
# call-time integration test), the repository matches, and the vulnerable
# construct is present at its parent, 74cd11c. No corpus correction.
#
# Vocabulary was checked for reuse before anything was written.
# code.LlmToolRegistrationMissingAuthorization (VYQL-AUTH-112) is a tool
# *registry* with no caller-privilege check, not a restriction enforced at the
# wrong layer; code.McpToolSchemaSecretParameter and
# code.McpToolCommandTemplateExecution are different weaknesses on the same
# surface; core.AllowlistValidation neutralises only command injection and SSRF
# and widening it was refused at ranks 1046, 1051 and 1216. No rule ID or concept
# is minted here, because nothing discriminating can be built on top of one --
# see below.
#
# What fires today. `timeout 180 ./bin/vyql scan --cache off --max-ram 6GB` over
# the vulnerable checkout reports 42 findings and 1 flag; nothing addresses this
# weakness. Nine of the 42 do read src/index.ts:212 -- `request.params` is already
# labelled code.HttpInput -- but every one of them is tool-*argument* taint
# (VYQL-DESER-001 into kubectl-get.ts:504, VYQL-INJ-013 into the secret-masking
# walker, VYQL-INJ-009 into log formatting) reported in the tool implementations,
# not the tool *name* reaching a dispatch it was not authorized for. The single
# flag is code.ResourceReview on child_process.spawn in port_forward.ts. Findings
# land in source; the repository ships no built dist/.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap: JavaScript has no cross-procedural view of a
# neutralising control, and no scope between a function body and whole-module
# text. Three separate things were measured, each against both revisions of the
# real src/index.ts with `-flags only`, emitting code.RequestScopePolicyReview
# (CWE_863, the nearest existing vehicle) from a throwaway javascript binding.
#
# 1. The handler cannot be identified as the tools/call handler. A lambda passed
#    as a call argument gets `call_method:<enclosing method>` in its function
#    context, so in a stripped repro the handler carries
#    `call_method:setRequestHandler`. In the real file the handler is wrapped --
#    `server.setRequestHandler(CallToolRequestSchema, withTelemetry(async (request) => {...}))`
#    -- and its only enclosing-call fact is `call_method:withTelemetry`, a
#    project-local middleware name. Keying on `call_method:setRequestHandler`
#    reports one flag on the real file, at index.ts:186 -- the tools/*list*
#    handler, which is unwrapped -- and nothing at the vulnerable handler. Which
#    schema a handler is registered for is a call argument, and per rank 1360 the
#    JavaScript frontend emits no call-argument tokens at all.
#
# 2. Function scope cannot see the fix. Dumping `str_args` of the
#    `analysis.function.context` node for the tools/call handler on both
#    revisions and diffing them, the *entire* difference is four tokens:
#    `call:isToolAllowed`, `call_path:isToolAllowed`, `identifier:isToolAllowed`
#    and the literal of the rejection message. Every fact about the decision
#    itself -- the Set membership test, the readonly and destructive list
#    lookups, the env reads -- is in a sibling arrow function and in module
#    scope, invisible from the handler. So:
#      node.scope == "function" and node.context.selector contains "request.params"
#      and node.context.identifier == "name"
#      and not (containsAny(node.context.callPath, [".has", ".includes", ".some", ".indexOf", ".find"]))
#    reports 1 flag on the vulnerable revision (index.ts:207) and 1 flag on the
#    fixed revision (index.ts:211) -- the same handler, identical output, no
#    discrimination. Adding "Allowed"/"allowed"/"authorize"/"permit" to the
#    negation does drop the fixed revision to zero, but that is a substring of
#    the fixing commit's own predicate name: a fix reading
#    `if (!withinConfiguredToolSet(name))` scores as unpatched, which is the
#    failure ranks 1364 and the four-variant measurement there already recorded.
#
# 3. Function scope also cannot see that a restriction exists at all, which is
#    what makes the finding a finding. An MCP server with no tool-restriction
#    feature has nothing to bypass, and its tools/call handler is *correctly*
#    ungated -- but the two are indistinguishable, because the env reads and the
#    filtered catalogue live in module scope. The variant in (2) reports 5 flags
#    of its own over the whole repository (the scan totals 6; the sixth is the
#    pre-existing code.ResourceReview on port_forward.ts): the true one at
#    src/index.ts:207 plus src/prompts/index.ts:32 (the prompts/get handler,
#    which has no allowlist because prompts are not restricted at all),
#    src/prompts/index.ts:5 (registerPromptHandlers, the exported wrapper that
#    merely contains that handler -- a function context inherits its nested
#    functions' tokens, so one handler is charged twice) and tests/sse.test.ts:80
#    and :103, both inside test fixtures. One true, four false, on the
#    repository the CVE is in. Over modelcontextprotocol/servers (65 JavaScript
#    and TypeScript files, 14 Python) it reports 0, but that is not evidence of
#    precision: those servers use the SDK's high-level `server.registerTool(name,
#    config, handler)` form, one handler per tool, so they have no central
#    `tools/call` dispatch function for a binding to sit on at all. The corpus
#    that would show the cost is low-level `setRequestHandler` servers, and this
#    repository is the one of those measured -- 1 true, 4 false. Narrowing the
#    positive further to the `name === "..."` if-chain (the real handler mixes an
#    if-chain and a trailing `switch (name)`) would be matching one codebase's
#    dispatch style rather than the weakness.
#
# Module scope, the only scope that holds both halves, cannot discriminate
# either, and not merely because whole-file facts combine by co-membership. Both
# revisions' module contexts carry `call_path:destructiveTools.some` and a
# `.includes`/`.filter` membership test over tool names -- the vulnerable one
# because the tools/list handler tests `allowedToolNames.includes(tool.name)`.
# The semantic difference is *which value* is tested: the vulnerable module only
# ever compares a catalogue element against another catalogue element
# (`expr:dt.name===tool.name`), while the fix compares catalogue elements against
# the requested scalar (`expr:t.name===name`, `expr:dt.name===name`) and, on its
# primary branch, calls `explicitlyAllowedToolNames.has(name)` -- a Set lookup
# that emits no `expr:` token and, again per rank 1360, no call-argument token.
# Measured:
#   node.scope == "module" and node.context.identifier == "CallToolRequestSchema"
#   and node.context.identifier == "ListToolsRequestSchema"
#   and node.context.call == "setRequestHandler"
# matches both revisions (1 flag each at index.ts:1); adding the membership-test
# negation takes both to 0. Identical either way.
#
# Behaviour a shippable fact needs, in order of how much it unblocks:
#  a. JavaScript call-argument tokens (rank 1360's gap, `jsStructuredContextTokens`
#     in internal/extract/frontend/treesitter/javascript.go), so that a handler
#     lambda can be identified by the request schema it is registered for and a
#     membership test can be attributed to the value it tests.
#  b. The structured context tokens of an intra-file resolved callee attributed,
#     one hop and bounded, to the calling function's context -- the same
#     behaviour rank 1364 needed for Rust, and here strictly narrower: unlike
#     that Rust case the hop already resolves. `vyql query -call isToolAllowed`
#     over the fixed file returns both call sites (index.ts:205 and :218) and
#     neither appears in `vyql resolve`'s unresolved list, so the callee body is
#     reachable; what is missing is only that its facts are not attributed to the
#     caller's `analysis.function.context`, which is where a binding reads them.
# Only with both does "the restriction the tools/list handler applies is not
# applied by the tools/call handler" become expressible without naming this
# fix's predicate.
#
# The four cases below are the minimal pair, all four rejected today. They differ
# only in where the allowlist decision sits: absent, delegated to a sibling
# predicate, written inline in the handler, and absent-but-correct because the
# server has no restriction to enforce. The delegated form is what the fix
# shipped, and the fourth is the false positive any function-scoped shape buys.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-engine-gap, all four forms rejected.

test "rank1372: an MCP tools/call handler dispatches a client-named tool with the configured tool restriction applied only to tools/list"
  lang javascript
  file src/index.ts
  reject_review code.RequestScopePolicyReview
  code
  ```
  const allowOnlyReadonlyTools = process.env.ALLOW_ONLY_READONLY_TOOLS === "true";
  const allowedToolsEnv = process.env.ALLOWED_TOOLS;
  const nonDestructiveTools =
    process.env.ALLOW_ONLY_NON_DESTRUCTIVE_TOOLS === "true";

  const readonlyTools = [kubectlGetSchema, kubectlDescribeSchema, kubectlLogsSchema];
  const destructiveTools = [kubectlDeleteSchema, uninstallHelmChartSchema, cleanupSchema];
  const allTools = [
    kubectlGetSchema,
    kubectlDescribeSchema,
    kubectlLogsSchema,
    kubectlDeleteSchema,
    uninstallHelmChartSchema,
    cleanupSchema,
  ];

  server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => {
    let tools;

    if (allowedToolsEnv) {
      const allowedToolNames = allowedToolsEnv.split(",").map((t) => t.trim());
      tools = allTools.filter((tool) => allowedToolNames.includes(tool.name));
    } else if (allowOnlyReadonlyTools) {
      tools = readonlyTools;
    } else if (nonDestructiveTools) {
      tools = allTools.filter(
        (tool) => !destructiveTools.some((dt) => dt.name === tool.name)
      );
    } else {
      tools = allTools;
    }

    return { tools };
  });

  server.setRequestHandler(
    CallToolRequestSchema,
    withTelemetry(async (request: {
      params: { name: string; _meta?: any; arguments?: Record<string, any> };
      method: string;
    }) => {
      try {
        const { name, arguments: input = {} } = request.params;

        if (name === "kubectl_get") {
          return await kubectlGet(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_describe") {
          return await kubectlDescribe(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_delete") {
          return await kubectlDelete(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "uninstall_helm_chart") {
          return await uninstallHelmChart(input as { name: string; namespace: string });
        }

        switch (name) {
          case "cleanup": {
            await k8sManager.cleanup();
            return { content: [{ type: "text", text: "cleaned up" }] };
          }

          default:
            throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InvalidRequest, `Unknown tool: ${name}`);
        }
      } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof McpError) throw error;
        throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InternalError, `Tool execution failed: ${error}`);
      }
    })
  );
  ```

test "rank1372: the same handler gated by a tool allowlist predicate factored into a sibling function"
  lang javascript
  file src/index.ts
  reject_review code.RequestScopePolicyReview
  code
  ```
  const allowOnlyReadonlyTools = process.env.ALLOW_ONLY_READONLY_TOOLS === "true";
  const allowedToolsEnv = process.env.ALLOWED_TOOLS;
  const nonDestructiveTools =
    process.env.ALLOW_ONLY_NON_DESTRUCTIVE_TOOLS === "true";

  const explicitlyAllowedToolNames = allowedToolsEnv
    ? new Set(allowedToolsEnv.split(",").map((t) => t.trim()).filter(Boolean))
    : null;

  const isToolAllowed = (name: string): boolean => {
    if (explicitlyAllowedToolNames) {
      return explicitlyAllowedToolNames.has(name);
    }
    if (allowOnlyReadonlyTools) {
      return readonlyTools.some((t) => t.name === name);
    }
    if (nonDestructiveTools) {
      return !destructiveTools.some((dt) => dt.name === name);
    }
    return true;
  };

  const readonlyTools = [kubectlGetSchema, kubectlDescribeSchema, kubectlLogsSchema];
  const destructiveTools = [kubectlDeleteSchema, uninstallHelmChartSchema, cleanupSchema];
  const allTools = [
    kubectlGetSchema,
    kubectlDescribeSchema,
    kubectlLogsSchema,
    kubectlDeleteSchema,
    uninstallHelmChartSchema,
    cleanupSchema,
  ];

  server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => {
    const baseTools = allowOnlyReadonlyTools ? readonlyTools : allTools;
    const tools = baseTools.filter((tool) => isToolAllowed(tool.name));
    return { tools };
  });

  server.setRequestHandler(
    CallToolRequestSchema,
    withTelemetry(async (request: {
      params: { name: string; _meta?: any; arguments?: Record<string, any> };
      method: string;
    }) => {
      try {
        const { name, arguments: input = {} } = request.params;

        if (!isToolAllowed(name)) {
          throw new McpError(
            ErrorCode.InvalidRequest,
            `Tool '${name}' is not allowed under the current server configuration`
          );
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_get") {
          return await kubectlGet(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_describe") {
          return await kubectlDescribe(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_delete") {
          return await kubectlDelete(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "uninstall_helm_chart") {
          return await uninstallHelmChart(input as { name: string; namespace: string });
        }

        switch (name) {
          case "cleanup": {
            await k8sManager.cleanup();
            return { content: [{ type: "text", text: "cleaned up" }] };
          }

          default:
            throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InvalidRequest, `Unknown tool: ${name}`);
        }
      } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof McpError) throw error;
        throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InternalError, `Tool execution failed: ${error}`);
      }
    })
  );
  ```

test "rank1372: the same allowlist decision written inline in the tools/call handler"
  lang javascript
  file src/index.ts
  reject_review code.RequestScopePolicyReview
  code
  ```
  const allowOnlyReadonlyTools = process.env.ALLOW_ONLY_READONLY_TOOLS === "true";
  const allowedToolsEnv = process.env.ALLOWED_TOOLS;

  const readonlyTools = [kubectlGetSchema, kubectlDescribeSchema, kubectlLogsSchema];
  const allTools = [
    kubectlGetSchema,
    kubectlDescribeSchema,
    kubectlLogsSchema,
    kubectlDeleteSchema,
    uninstallHelmChartSchema,
    cleanupSchema,
  ];

  server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => {
    const tools = allowOnlyReadonlyTools ? readonlyTools : allTools;
    return { tools };
  });

  server.setRequestHandler(
    CallToolRequestSchema,
    withTelemetry(async (request: {
      params: { name: string; _meta?: any; arguments?: Record<string, any> };
      method: string;
    }) => {
      try {
        const { name, arguments: input = {} } = request.params;

        if (allowedToolsEnv) {
          const allowedToolNames = allowedToolsEnv.split(",").map((t) => t.trim());
          if (!allowedToolNames.includes(name)) {
            throw new McpError(
              ErrorCode.InvalidRequest,
              `Tool '${name}' is not allowed under the current server configuration`
            );
          }
        } else if (allowOnlyReadonlyTools && !readonlyTools.some((t) => t.name === name)) {
          throw new McpError(
            ErrorCode.InvalidRequest,
            `Tool '${name}' is not allowed under the current server configuration`
          );
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_get") {
          return await kubectlGet(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_delete") {
          return await kubectlDelete(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        switch (name) {
          case "cleanup": {
            await k8sManager.cleanup();
            return { content: [{ type: "text", text: "cleaned up" }] };
          }

          default:
            throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InvalidRequest, `Unknown tool: ${name}`);
        }
      } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof McpError) throw error;
        throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InternalError, `Tool execution failed: ${error}`);
      }
    })
  );
  ```

test "rank1372: an MCP server with no tool restriction to enforce dispatches the tools it advertises"
  lang javascript
  file src/prompts/index.ts
  reject_review code.RequestScopePolicyReview
  code
  ```
  const allTools = [kubectlGetSchema, kubectlDescribeSchema, kubectlLogsSchema];

  server.setRequestHandler(ListToolsRequestSchema, async () => {
    return { tools: allTools };
  });

  server.setRequestHandler(
    CallToolRequestSchema,
    withTelemetry(async (request: {
      params: { name: string; _meta?: any; arguments?: Record<string, any> };
      method: string;
    }) => {
      try {
        const { name, arguments: input = {} } = request.params;

        if (name === "kubectl_get") {
          return await kubectlGet(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        if (name === "kubectl_describe") {
          return await kubectlDescribe(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
        }

        switch (name) {
          case "kubectl_logs": {
            return await kubectlLogs(k8sManager, input as { resourceType: string });
          }

          default:
            throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InvalidRequest, `Unknown tool: ${name}`);
        }
      } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof McpError) throw error;
        throw new McpError(ErrorCode.InternalError, `Tool execution failed: ${error}`);
      }
    })
  );
  ```
